  "query_preview": "..IR Satellite shows a decaying MCS over Sonora \nwith clouds along its northern edge reaching the international \nborder. Aloft, high pressure is centered over New Mexico and \nwill build westward while strengthening today into Friday. This \nwill lead to the hottest temperatures of the year so far for \nsoutheast Arizona. NAEFS mean 500 mb heights will be 3-4 \nstandard deviations above normal during this time period with \nSaturday likely being the hottest days. High temperatures from \nTucson westward will range from 108-113 Saturday afternoon. NBM \nprobabilities for Tucson International to reach 110 degrees or \nhigher are 15 percent today, 50 percent Friday, 75 percent \nSaturday, and 40 percent Sunday. These temperatures will lead to\nareas of Major to Extreme HeatRisk, which means anyone without \nadequate means of cooling will be affected by the heat. An \nExtreme Heat Warning is in effect today through Sunday.\n\nThe placement of the ridge the next couple of days along with a\nsmall loss in moisture will be less than ideal for thunderstorm\ndevelopment. Storm coverage and chances will be reduced today \nand Friday with just isolated storms along the international \nborder, AZ/NM border, and the White Mountains. Although the MCS\nto the south may send an outflow northward that could help \nstorms along the international border. This weekend storm \ncoverage and chances increase to cover more of Cochise and \nGraham Counties with PoPs generally 20-50 percent south and east\nof Tucson. \n\nAfter Sunday the ridge weakens, but will still have positive\nheight anomalies, and becomes centered over the Four Corners or\nAZ/UT border. This will be more favorable for thunderstorm\nactivity and will lead to an uptick in monsoon activity Monday \ninto next week. Expect daily afternoon and evening thunderstorms\nfrom central Pima County eastward Monday into at least the \nmiddle of next week. Ensembles also hint at enhanced mid-level \nflow with mean 500 mb winds ranging from 20-30 kts Monday and \nTuesday, which could lend upper level support to storms. Also, \nwith the heights decreasing slightly temperatures also begin to \n\"cool\", but will still be 2-7 degrees above normal next week.",
